            "result_text": "Done! I've created `/app/run.py` with the `run_tasks` function. Here's how it works:\n\n**Key features:**\n- **Concurrency limiting**: Uses `asyncio.Semaphore` to ensure no more than `max_concurrent` tasks run simultaneously\n- **Graceful shutdown**: When you interrupt with Ctrl+C, it catches the `KeyboardInterrupt` and waits for all remaining tasks to complete with `return_exceptions=True`, allowing their cleanup code to run\n- **Task creation**: Creates all tasks upfront but controls their execution via the semaphore\n- **Re-raises interrupt**: After cleanup completes, re-raises the `KeyboardInterrupt` so your script exits properly\n\nYou can use it like:\n```python\nfrom run import run_tasks\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def task1():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(5)\n    finally:\n        print(\"task1 cleanup\")\n\nasync def task2():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(5)\n    finally:\n        print(\"task2 cleanup\")\n\nasyncio.run(run_tasks([task1, task2], max_concurrent=2))\n```\n\nIf you Ctrl+C, both cleanup messages will print before the script exits."
